        <div id="wattage" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Wattage: What the Number Actually Buys
          </h2>

          <p>
            Wattage tells you how fast the soleplate heats up and, more importantly, how fast it recovers when steam and damp fabric pull heat out of it. A low-wattage iron feels fine on the first shirt and sluggish by the fourth, because every burst of steam cools the plate and the element has to catch up. That is why we treat 1,500 watts as the floor for a main household iron and 1,700 to 1,800 as the sweet spot.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The 1,800 Watt Ceiling</h3>

          <p>
            There is a hard ceiling in North America. A standard 15-amp, 120-volt household circuit tops out at 1,800 watts, which is why US irons cluster between 1,500 and 1,800 and almost never go higher. When you see European reviews of the Philips Azur range quoting 2,400 to 3,000 watts, those are 220 to 240 volt irons built for that grid. They are not sold here as 120-volt models, and a higher number on a foreign spec sheet does not translate to a US iron.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Mind the Circuit</h3>

          <p>
            That ceiling also means you should not run a 1,800 watt iron on the same circuit as a space heater, a hair dryer or a toaster oven. If the breaker trips mid-shirt, that is why. Plug the iron directly into a wall outlet rather than a thin extension cord; if you must use one, it should be rated for the full current the iron draws.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Diminishing Returns Above 1,700 Watts</h3>

          <p>
            Above about 1,700 watts the returns flatten out. The difference between the 1,725 watt Rowenta Focus and the 1,800 watt Perfect Steam Pro is not the extra 75 watts, it is the separate boiler that the steam station uses. For a handheld iron, spend your money on the soleplate and steam system once you are past 1,600 watts.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="steam" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Steam Output: Continuous g/min and the Shot
          </h2>

          <p>
            Steam is what actually relaxes fibers so wrinkles release; the heat alone mostly just flattens them. Makers express it two ways. Continuous steam, in grams per minute, is the steady flow while you hold the iron flat. The steam shot or burst is a short, much larger blast you trigger with a button for stubborn creases in cotton and linen. Both matter, but continuous steam does most of the work across a full load.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Missing Steam Figures</h3>

          <p>
            Here is the frustrating part: many US iron listings do not publish a continuous steam figure at all. Rowenta's European listings for Focus Excel models quote roughly 40 to 45 grams per minute, but the US DW5280 page leans on hole count instead. Black+Decker says only that the Allure produces over 30 percent more steam than competing models, without a number. When a figure is missing, treat hole count and wattage as rough proxies and read owner reports about steam on heavy fabrics.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Steam Station Output</h3>

          <p>
            Steam stations are a different class. The Rowenta Perfect Steam Pro is rated for 80 grams per minute of continuous steam and a 430 gram per minute boost at 7.4 bar of pressure, because it heats water in a separate boiler instead of dripping it onto a hot plate. That is why a station can push steam through a doubled seam or a thick canvas jacket that a regular iron only skims.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Vertical Steam</h3>

          <p>
            Vertical steam is the other spec worth checking. It lets you hold the iron upright and steam a hanging garment or curtain. Most of the irons here offer it, but a handheld iron's vertical steam is only as strong as its shot, so for regular hanging-garment work a dedicated steamer or station does it better.
          </p>
        </div>

