          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Stainless Steel</h3>

          <p>
            Stainless steel is our default recommendation because it lasts. Rowenta's laser-drilled Microsteam plates spread 400 small holes across the whole sole, which puts steam right up to the tip instead of concentrating it in a few big holes near the heel. Stainless also tolerates the occasional brush with a metal zipper that would gouge a nonstick coating.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Ceramic</h3>

          <p>
            Ceramic plates, including CHI's titanium-infused version, glide beautifully and heat evenly, and owners who iron a lot of synthetics and delicates tend to like them. The tradeoff is that a ceramic coating can chip if the iron is dropped, and once it chips it can snag fabric. Nonstick plates are cheap and fine at first, but they are the first to scratch and lose their glide. Avoid them on a main iron.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="auto-off" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Auto Shut-Off and Safety
          </h2>

          <p>
            An iron left on is one of the classic household fire and burn risks, and auto shut-off is the single safety feature we would not compromise on. The good version is three-way: the iron switches off after a short time if it is left face down or tipped on its side, and after a longer time if it is left standing on its heel. Black+Decker publishes the exact timings for the Allure D3030: 30 seconds on the soleplate or sides and 8 minutes on the heel.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How Three-Way Shut-Off Works</h3>

          <p>
            Rowenta's three-way smart shut-off on its Focus line follows the same pattern, with retailer listings giving 30 seconds horizontal or tipped and 8 minutes upright. CHI lists auto shut-off on the 13102 but timings vary between CHI models and retailer listings, so check the manual in the box. Steam stations shut off too, but usually after a longer idle period because the boiler takes time to reheat.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">A Backstop, Not a Substitute</h3>

          <p>
            Auto shut-off is not a reason to leave an iron unattended. It is a backstop for the morning you get distracted, not a substitute for unplugging. Keep the cord where children and pets cannot pull it, never fill the tank while the iron is plugged in, and let the iron cool fully on its heel before storing it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Steamers Often Lack It</h3>

          <p>
            One gap to know about: handheld garment steamers often do not have an auto shut-off at all. We could not find one listed for the Rowenta X-Cel Steam+, which is common for steamers. Unplug a steamer the moment you finish with it, and set it down on its base, not on a bed or a towel.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="water" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Tap Water, Hard Water and Anti-Calc Systems
          </h2>

          <p>
            Scale, the mineral crust left behind when hard water boils off, is what kills most steam irons. It clogs steam vents, spits white flakes onto dark clothes and eventually blocks the steam chamber. How an iron handles your water matters more than almost any other spec if you live in a hard-water area.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Tap Water Is Usually Fine</h3>

          <p>
            Most current irons are designed for ordinary tap water. Rowenta's DW5280 manual says it is built to run on untreated tap water, while still telling you to run the self-clean cycle regularly to flush mineral deposits. Rowenta manuals for other models add that if your water is very hard, a 50-50 mix of tap water and distilled water or bottled spring water reduces scale. Some makers advise against pure distilled or demineralized water in certain models, so follow the manual rather than assuming distilled is always safer.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Water to Avoid</h3>

          <p>
            Rowenta manuals also typically warn against water from a clothes dryer, scented water, and water with additives. If you have a whole-house softener, it is worth checking your iron's manual for its stance on softened water. Our guide to the <a href="/best-water-softener-system/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">best water softener systems</a> covers why softened water behaves differently from plain hard water.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How Anti-Calc Systems Differ</h3>

          <p>
            Anti-calc designs vary. The Rowenta Everlast DW7180 is built around a calc collector that traps loose scale particles so you can rinse them out. The Perfect Steam Pro station uses a removable anti-scale collector and a scale alert. Simpler irons, like the Black+Decker Allure, rely on a self-clean cycle you run by hand. All of them only work if you actually use them, so set a reminder every few weeks.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="iron-vs-steamer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Iron vs Steam Station vs Handheld Steamer
          </h2>

          <p>
            These three tools overlap, but they are good at different jobs. A regular steam iron is the cheapest, most versatile choice and the only one that puts a sharp crease in trousers or a crisp collar on a shirt. A steam station is an iron connected by a hose to a separate boiler, which delivers far more steam for big loads. A handheld steamer never touches the fabric and is fastest for quick de-wrinkling of hanging clothes.
          </p>

