          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When a Steam Station Makes Sense</h3>

          <p>
            A steam station makes sense if you iron more than about five shirts at a sitting, or regularly press tablecloths and bed linen. The big removable tank, 37 ounces on the Perfect Steam Pro, means you are not refilling every ten minutes, and the higher pressure finishes each garment in fewer passes. The downsides are a large footprint on the ironing board, a longer heat-up and a much higher price than a good iron.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When a Handheld Steamer Is Enough</h3>

          <p>
            A handheld steamer is the right call for people who own few cotton shirts and lots of knits, dresses and suiting. It also sanitizes and refreshes upholstery and drapes. If you are thinking about bigger cleaning jobs with steam, such as grout and floors, that is a different machine; our guide to the <a href="/best-steam-cleaner/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">best steam cleaners</a> covers it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Laundry Habits Change the Answer</h3>

          <p>
            Laundry habits change the answer too. If you dry shirts on a hanger or pull them from the dryer the moment it stops, a steamer may be all you need. A gentler dryer helps here; our <a href="/best-heat-pump-dryer/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">heat pump dryer guide</a> explains why lower drying temperatures leave fewer baked-in wrinkles.
          </p>
