        <div id="not-worth-it" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. What's Not Worth Buying
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Irons Without Auto Shut-Off</h3>

          <p>
            Skip any iron without automatic shut-off. A handful of very cheap irons still omit it, and the savings are a few dollars against a real fire risk. The same goes for unbranded irons on marketplaces that list no UL or ETL safety certification mark.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Discontinued Favorites</h3>

          <p>
            Be wary of discontinued favorites. The Shark Ultimate Professional GI505 still appears on older best-of lists, but new stock has largely dried up and what remains is mostly renewed units. The Steamfast SF-717 mini iron was a long-time travel pick, but older dual-voltage Steamfast Home and Away irons, including the SF-717, were covered by a Vornado recall, so do not buy one used without checking the recall notice and its manufacture date.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">European-Spec Irons</h3>

          <p>
            Do not pay extra for a European spec sheet. Philips Azur irons get excellent reviews abroad, but the models rated at 2,400 to 3,000 watts are 220 to 240 volt products. Imported units will not run safely on a US outlet, and a voltage converter strong enough for a 2,400 watt iron is not a sensible purchase.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Overbuying</h3>

          <p>
            Finally, do not overbuy. A steam station is a superb tool for a heavy ironer and an expensive closet ornament for everyone else. If you iron once a week, the Rowenta Focus or the Black+Decker Allure will do the job well for years. Put the savings toward the appliances that do more daily work, like a good <a href="/best-vacuum-cleaner/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">vacuum cleaner</a> or a reliable fridge from our <a href="/best-refrigerator-brands/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">refrigerator brands guide</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many watts should a good electric iron have?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a main household iron, look for 1,500 to 1,800 watts. More wattage helps the soleplate stay hot and recover between bursts of steam, which matters on a full load of cotton shirts. In the US, 1,800 watts is the practical ceiling for a 120-volt iron, so the 2,400 to 3,000 watt figures you see in European reviews do not apply here.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a stainless steel or ceramic soleplate better?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Stainless steel is the more durable choice and resists scratches from zippers and buttons, which is why it is our default. Ceramic, including titanium-infused ceramic like CHI's, glides more smoothly and heats evenly, and owners who iron lots of synthetics like it. Ceramic can chip if dropped, though. Nonstick plates are cheapest but wear out first.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use tap water in my steam iron?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most current irons are designed for ordinary tap water; Rowenta's DW5280 manual says it runs on untreated tap water. If your water is very hard, Rowenta suggests mixing tap water half and half with distilled or bottled spring water. Avoid dryer water, scented water and additives, and check your manual, because some makers advise against pure distilled water.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What does grams per minute mean on an iron?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Grams per minute measures how much steam the iron puts out. Continuous steam is the steady flow while you iron, while the steam shot or boost is a short, much bigger blast for stubborn creases. A steam station like the Rowenta Perfect Steam Pro is rated for 80 grams per minute continuous and a 430 gram per minute boost, far above typical irons.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a steam station worth it over a regular iron?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only if you iron a lot. A steam station heats water in a separate boiler, so it delivers much more steam and pressure and a bigger tank. That saves real time on weekly piles of shirts and bed linen. For a few garments a week, a good iron is cheaper, faster to heat and far easier to store.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a handheld steamer a replacement for an iron?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For some wardrobes, yes. A steamer is quicker for dresses, suits, knits and curtains on a hanger, and it cannot scorch fabric. But it cannot put a sharp crease in trousers or a crisp finish on a cotton collar. Many people end up owning both: an iron for shirts and a steamer for everything else.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long should a steam iron last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A decent iron used weekly commonly lasts several years, and scale is the most common reason it fails early. Running the self-clean cycle regularly, emptying the tank after each use and using the right water for your area all extend its life. A stainless soleplate also outlasts nonstick, which tends to scratch and lose its glide first.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ironing everything on the hottest setting</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Start with the most delicate fabric in the pile and work up to cotton and linen. A plate set for linen will melt synthetics and leave shiny marks on dark wool. Sorting by heat also means the iron is never waiting to cool down, so a full load goes faster and nothing gets scorched.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Never running the self-clean cycle</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Scale builds up silently until the iron starts spitting white flakes or the steam fades. Rowenta's manuals for its Focus line suggest a self-clean roughly every two weeks, and more often in hard-water areas. Five minutes over the sink every few weeks adds years to an iron's life and keeps dark clothes clean.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving water in the tank between sessions</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Water left sitting in a warm iron leaves more mineral deposits behind and can leak onto the board or the shelf. Empty the tank while the iron is still warm, then store it upright on its heel. It is a small habit that keeps the steam vents clear and stops drips on the next shirt.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using scented or dryer water</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Water from a tumble dryer, fragranced ironing water and liquids with additives can clog steam vents and stain fabric, and Rowenta's manuals typically warn against them. Plain tap water, or a tap and distilled mix if your water is very hard, is what most current irons are built for. Check your own manual first.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a steam station for occasional ironing</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A station is brilliant for weekly piles of shirts and a waste of money and cupboard space for a couple of garments a week. It takes longer to heat up, needs room on the board and costs several times a good iron. If you mostly touch up clothes, a quality iron or a handheld steamer is the smarter buy.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

